= Compositionality =

== Overview ==
'''Compositionality''' is the principle that the meaning of a complex expression is determined systematically by the meanings of its parts and the way those parts are combined.

It helps explain how speakers can understand novel expressions rather than memorizing every possible sentence.

== Structure matters ==
Compositionality is not simple word-by-word substitution.

Compare:

<blockquote>The dog chased the cat.</blockquote>

<blockquote>The cat chased the dog.</blockquote>

The words are nearly the same, but [[Syntax|syntactic]] structure and grammatical roles change the interpretation.

A compositional theory therefore maps structured expressions, not just bags of words, to meanings.

== Formal idea ==
If a complex expression E is formed from parts e₁ ... eₙ by a syntactic operation R, a compositional semantics supplies an interpretation rule F such that:

<pre>
⟦E⟧ = F_R(⟦e₁⟧, ..., ⟦eₙ⟧)
</pre>

The exact definition of “meaning”, “part”, “structure”, and “determined” varies across theories.

== Challenges ==
Natural language contains phenomena that complicate naive compositional accounts: idioms, [[Polysemy|polysemy]], coercion, ellipsis, anaphora, metaphor, quotation, context-sensitive expressions, presupposition, and implicature.

These do not automatically refute compositionality. Theories may enrich lexical representations, syntactic structure, semantic types, or contextual parameters.

== Context ==
Compositionality does not require interpretation to be independent of [[Context (linguistics)|context]].

A theory can compositionally determine a context-sensitive function or combine contextual parameters with lexical and structural information.

== Why it matters ==
Compositionality is central to formal semantics, logic, grammar engineering, semantic parsing, and some approaches to [[Meaning representation|meaning representation]].

It also raises philosophical questions about learnability, productivity, systematicity, and the relation between linguistic form and thought.

